WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:02.293
- What do you see when
you look at this picture?

00:00:02.293 --> 00:00:04.546
Hopefully it's either a duck or a rabbit.

00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:07.632
Most of us can see both,
but not at the same time.

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:09.592
We sort of switch back and forth.

00:00:09.592 --> 00:00:11.678
This is known as an ambiguous figure.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:13.847
You've probably seen lots of them.

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:17.142
And they're often used by
psychologists and neuroscientists

00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:19.686
to study things like visual
perception and cognition.

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:21.938
Recent studies have found
that bilingual children,

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:24.065
those that speak two languages fluently,

00:00:24.065 --> 00:00:26.359
are more adept at switching back and forth

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:29.029
between such images than
monolingual children.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:30.363
For a long time,

00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:32.741
people thought that
bilingualism was a handicap,

00:00:32.741 --> 00:00:35.994
that it stunted development
and language acquisition.

00:00:35.994 --> 00:00:37.996
Maybe that's because no
one really studied it

00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:39.456
until recently.

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:41.166
Over the last several decades,

00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:43.334
research and experiments have suggested

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:46.671
that being bilingual significantly
impacts brain function

00:00:46.671 --> 00:00:48.673
in ways that might surprise you.

00:00:48.673 --> 00:00:51.634
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky,
and this is Otherwords.

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:54.220
(quirky music)

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:00.143
- [Narrator] Otherwords.

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:02.854
(quirky music)

00:01:02.854 --> 00:01:04.481
- Do you want to try and put that on?

00:01:04.481 --> 00:01:05.857
- [Erica] Dr. Maria Arredondo

00:01:05.857 --> 00:01:07.442
is a developmental psychologist

00:01:07.442 --> 00:01:09.819
who studies the cognitive
impacts of bilingualism

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:11.154
in young children.

00:01:11.154 --> 00:01:13.239
- I'm an assistant
professor in the Department

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.283
of Human Development and Family Sciences

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:17.577
at the University of Texas at Austin.

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:20.622
And I am the director of the child's lab.

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:23.083
We study how bilingual children

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:25.585
and infants are acquiring their languages

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:26.836
and what are the mechanisms

00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:29.839
that are supporting the
acquisition of both languages.

00:01:29.839 --> 00:01:33.384
- So what drew you to the field
of bilingualism in general?

00:01:33.384 --> 00:01:36.846
- So I was born in Argentina,
my first language is Spanish,

00:01:36.846 --> 00:01:39.557
and I moved to the United
States when I was 12.

00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:42.644
I acquire a language very
quickly, in one or two years,

00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:45.730
but my siblings and my
parents struggled a lot.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:48.066
So I always became curious

00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:52.195
why some people are learning
their languages very quickly

00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:53.988
and why are others seem to struggle

00:01:53.988 --> 00:01:56.449
in bilingual acquisition.

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:58.785
- [Erica] There are two
kinds of bilingualism,

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.246
simultaneous and sequential.

00:02:01.246 --> 00:02:03.540
Simultaneous bilinguals
learn both languages

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.750
at the same time in infancy

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.378
while sequentials learn
their dominant language first

00:02:08.378 --> 00:02:10.171
and a second later in life.

00:02:10.171 --> 00:02:12.841
While a sequential bilingual
may maintain a similar level

00:02:12.841 --> 00:02:16.094
of proficiency, there are
fundamental differences

00:02:16.094 --> 00:02:19.222
in how these languages
are acquired and used.

00:02:19.222 --> 00:02:21.724
One of the most significant is phonetics.

00:02:21.724 --> 00:02:22.767
It's estimated

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:26.229
that humans can make around
800 distinct verbal sounds,

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:30.483
known as phonemes, like ch or oo or ss.

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:33.486
Any individual language
will only use a few dozen,

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:36.156
and the ones you don't
use, you can't really hear.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:39.284
For instance, r and l may sound distinct

00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:40.451
to English speakers,

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:43.121
but the Japanese only
have one phoneme for both,

00:02:43.121 --> 00:02:46.124
and in French, there's
a sound between p and b

00:02:46.124 --> 00:02:47.834
that English speakers can't discern.

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.211
But here's the amazing thing.

00:02:50.211 --> 00:02:53.047
Babies can hear all of these sounds.

00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:54.382
- [Maria] So from very early on,

00:02:54.382 --> 00:02:56.718
infants can distinguish
the different sounds

00:02:56.718 --> 00:02:59.929
in all the languages, but with time,

00:02:59.929 --> 00:03:03.016
they begin to specialize in the sounds

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:05.059
for their own native language.

00:03:05.059 --> 00:03:06.477
- [Erica] Multiple studies suggest

00:03:06.477 --> 00:03:08.563
that infants under six months

00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:11.858
can perceive the whole
spectrum of phonemes.

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:14.402
As they develop, they
focus only on those used

00:03:14.402 --> 00:03:15.695
by their native language

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:18.323
and essentially lose the
ability to hear the rest.

00:03:18.323 --> 00:03:20.408
It's called perceptual narrowing.

00:03:20.408 --> 00:03:23.745
As you can guess, bilingual
children keep all the phonemes

00:03:23.745 --> 00:03:25.163
from both languages,

00:03:25.163 --> 00:03:27.790
but only if they learn both from infancy.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:28.875
Sequential bilinguals,

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:31.252
those who learned a second
language later in life,

00:03:31.252 --> 00:03:33.713
almost always retain a perceptible accent,

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:35.298
no matter how proficient they are

00:03:35.298 --> 00:03:37.759
in all other aspects of a language.

00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:40.511
There's pretty much just
that one narrow window

00:03:40.511 --> 00:03:42.013
to learn all the phonemes you'll use

00:03:42.013 --> 00:03:43.264
for the rest of your life.

00:03:43.264 --> 00:03:46.059
But if you're picturing all
these phonemes sloshing around

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:47.644
like a bowl of alphabet soup,

00:03:47.644 --> 00:03:50.355
it's actually a lot more
organized than that.

00:03:50.355 --> 00:03:53.608
So can children distinguish
between the two languages?

00:03:53.608 --> 00:03:56.444
- Bilingual-raised
children can distinguish

00:03:56.444 --> 00:03:59.239
between their two languages very early on,

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:01.241
during the first year of life.

00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:04.369
We know that they can do
that as soon as they're born.

00:04:04.369 --> 00:04:07.413
There are studies showing that neonates,

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:09.582
they can distinguish between the languages

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:13.753
that their mom spoke
while they were in utero.

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:15.630
As they're acquiring both languages,

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:19.717
they are beginning to
figure out on their own

00:04:19.717 --> 00:04:22.095
which sounds are for each language,

00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:26.849
but also what is the syntax
for each of their languages.

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:29.435
- The two languages are
very clearly separated

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:32.605
and delineated in a bilingual's
mind, which makes sense

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:35.775
since they have different
sounds, rules, and rhythms.

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:37.694
When they change from
one language to another,

00:04:37.694 --> 00:04:41.739
even mid-sentence, it's like
a mental switch is flipped.

00:04:41.739 --> 00:04:43.783
One language is given full attention

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:45.285
and the other one is turned off,

00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:46.953
so as not to cause confusion.

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:49.914
- [Maria] They're very aware
of their code-switching.

00:04:49.914 --> 00:04:51.791
So for instance, my niece,

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:54.877
she will mix between
both of their languages

00:04:54.877 --> 00:04:56.212
when she's speaking to me

00:04:56.212 --> 00:04:58.298
because she knows that I am bilingual

00:04:58.298 --> 00:05:01.050
and that I am capable of understanding

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.177
when she is code-switching,

00:05:03.177 --> 00:05:07.390
but she wouldn't do that to an
English monolingual speaker.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:09.684
- But how would an infant brain know

00:05:09.684 --> 00:05:12.061
that it's learning two
different languages?

00:05:12.061 --> 00:05:14.105
One possible answer is prosody,

00:05:14.105 --> 00:05:16.065
the patterns of rhythm in speech.

00:05:16.065 --> 00:05:17.066
For example,

00:05:17.066 --> 00:05:19.360
English is a stress-timed language,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.155
which means that the rhythm is
based on the important words

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:23.448
and all the unimportant words

00:05:23.448 --> 00:05:25.575
are just jammed in between the beats.

00:05:25.575 --> 00:05:27.577
I'm gonna go the store,
I'm gonna buy some snacks,

00:05:27.577 --> 00:05:28.953
and then I'll come home.

00:05:28.953 --> 00:05:31.622
Spanish, however, is a
syllable-timed language

00:05:31.622 --> 00:05:34.667
where each syllable is
given more equal timing.

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:39.714
Voy a ir al supermercado, a comprar unos bocadillos,
 y ya vuelvo de regreso.

00:05:40.214 --> 00:05:43.092
Prosody is also influenced by word order.

00:05:43.092 --> 00:05:47.013
English and Spanish are both
subject-verb-object languages.

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:48.598
The dog ate his bone.

00:05:48.598 --> 00:05:51.100
El perro se comió su hueso.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:54.187
SVO languages usually
have articles like "the"

00:05:54.187 --> 00:05:57.357
before the noun, giving it
an iambic sound pattern.

00:05:57.357 --> 00:05:58.941
The dog,

00:05:58.941 --> 00:05:59.776
el perro.

00:06:01.652 --> 00:06:02.653
My mother,

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:04.864
me madre.

00:06:06.282 --> 00:06:09.285
Subject-object-verb
languages, like Japanese,

00:06:09.285 --> 00:06:11.162
place the article after the noun,

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:14.040
which creates a somewhat
more inverse sound pattern.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:17.168
Kare ni

00:06:17.168 --> 00:06:20.004
Soko de

00:06:20.004 --> 00:06:22.757
- [Erica] It's possible
that even in the womb,

00:06:22.757 --> 00:06:26.386
a fetus may be able to pick
up on these distinct patterns

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:28.137
and start to sort linguistic information

00:06:28.137 --> 00:06:30.556
into separate mental containers.

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:32.683
How are the strategies
that bilingual children use

00:06:32.683 --> 00:06:34.936
to learn language different
from monolingual children?

00:06:34.936 --> 00:06:37.688
- We know that monolinguals,
around 18 months,

00:06:37.688 --> 00:06:42.193
they use mutual exclusivity,
which is a strategy

00:06:42.193 --> 00:06:47.198
in which they are mapping a
novel word to a novel object.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.451
So for instance, if you present
a child with two objects,

00:06:50.451 --> 00:06:53.955
one is a familiar object,
one is an unfamiliar object,

00:06:53.955 --> 00:06:57.250
and you provide a name that
they've never heard before,

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:00.253
then they're going to map that novel name

00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:02.130
to that new object.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:05.133
But bilinguals, there's evidence showing

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:07.802
that they're not relying
on mutual exclusivity

00:07:07.802 --> 00:07:09.595
as much as monolinguals.

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:13.474
And that's because they know
that two labels are given

00:07:13.474 --> 00:07:15.268
to one object.

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:18.396
There are other strategies
that bilinguals rely on

00:07:18.396 --> 00:07:19.522
when they're learning words,

00:07:19.522 --> 00:07:23.860
so we do know that they are
looking at audio-visual cues,

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.112
the mouth of the speaker.

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:27.822
The more that they look at the mouth,

00:07:27.822 --> 00:07:29.866
the higher their retention is

00:07:29.866 --> 00:07:33.661
when they are naming a new object.

00:07:33.661 --> 00:07:36.581
- As you can see, the mental
demands on bilingual children

00:07:36.581 --> 00:07:38.916
are pretty heavy, and it does mean

00:07:38.916 --> 00:07:41.252
that some language
milestones may show a delay

00:07:41.252 --> 00:07:43.129
when compared to a monolingual child,

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:45.882
which makes sense considering
that the developing brain

00:07:45.882 --> 00:07:48.759
of a bilingual child
has double the workload.

00:07:48.759 --> 00:07:50.595
But, like a bodybuilder,

00:07:50.595 --> 00:07:52.889
that extra effort means extra muscle,

00:07:52.889 --> 00:07:54.599
neurologically speaking.

00:07:54.599 --> 00:07:56.851
- [Maria] In the brain, we see

00:07:56.851 --> 00:08:00.188
that bilinguals are
showing different activity

00:08:00.188 --> 00:08:02.607
during executive function tasks.

00:08:02.607 --> 00:08:05.985
The mechanisms that we are relying on

00:08:05.985 --> 00:08:08.488
are attention and inhibition.

00:08:08.488 --> 00:08:12.575
So with attention, we are
focusing on the language

00:08:12.575 --> 00:08:14.452
that's being in use right now,

00:08:14.452 --> 00:08:18.915
so I'm focusing on English
and I'm inhibiting my Spanish.

00:08:18.915 --> 00:08:20.708
- [Erica] That's why
bilingual children are better

00:08:20.708 --> 00:08:23.294
at tests involving ambiguous figures.

00:08:23.294 --> 00:08:26.255
It's easier and faster for
them to turn off rabbit

00:08:26.255 --> 00:08:28.216
and turn on duck.

00:08:28.216 --> 00:08:29.050
- [Maria] So the better

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:31.427
your executive function abilities are,

00:08:31.427 --> 00:08:34.889
the better that your
academic abilities seem to be

00:08:34.889 --> 00:08:36.641
during the school years.

00:08:36.641 --> 00:08:38.392
What's fascinating about this work

00:08:38.392 --> 00:08:41.437
is that it's showing
that the brain is plastic

00:08:41.437 --> 00:08:44.857
and that experiences like
bilingualism can alter

00:08:44.857 --> 00:08:47.485
how the brain is organized.

00:08:47.485 --> 00:08:51.739
There is research showing that
bilingualism is associated

00:08:51.739 --> 00:08:55.993
with a delay in Alzheimer's diagnosis.

00:08:55.993 --> 00:09:00.164
With age, obviously, the
brain begins to deteriorate,

00:09:00.164 --> 00:09:02.166
but bilinguals are showing

00:09:02.166 --> 00:09:05.461
that this is a much slower deterioration.

00:09:05.461 --> 00:09:08.381
But what we still need
to try to understand

00:09:08.381 --> 00:09:11.717
is how these changes in
the brain are related

00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:14.053
to bilingual acquisition.

00:09:14.053 --> 00:09:16.889
And that's where the field is heading now.

00:09:16.889 --> 00:09:18.975
- That's not even mentioning the social

00:09:18.975 --> 00:09:21.185
and cultural benefits of bilingualism

00:09:21.185 --> 00:09:23.396
that are harder to test in a lab.

00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:25.481
The more people you can communicate with,

00:09:25.481 --> 00:09:27.984
the more opportunities life can offer

00:09:27.984 --> 00:09:30.611
and the richer your experiences can be.

00:09:30.611 --> 00:09:32.405
Unlike the cognitive advantages,

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:34.031
these are available to simultaneous

00:09:34.031 --> 00:09:35.575
and sequential bilinguals,

00:09:35.575 --> 00:09:37.326
so it's never too late to benefit

00:09:37.326 --> 00:09:39.745
from learning a second language.

00:09:39.745 --> 00:09:43.499
It's estimated that humans
are capable, capable, capable.

00:09:43.499 --> 00:09:46.752
It's estimated that
humans are capable, cape.

00:09:46.752 --> 00:09:49.422
It's estimated that humans are capable,

00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:50.590
I can't even say capable.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:51.591
Cape.

00:09:51.591 --> 00:09:52.675
Starting from scratch.

